\section{Introduction}
\label{sec:intro}

Consider estimating whether a content moderation policy reduces hate speech differently across demographic groups, using GPT-4 to classify thousands of posts.
After standard confusion-matrix correction, the analysis concludes the policy \emph{increases} hate speech among younger users---but the true effect is protective.
This sign reversal is not an edge case.

Large language models (LLMs) now routinely replace human annotators in computational social science, producing labels for sentiment, hate speech, stance, and political discourse at costs orders of magnitude below manual coding~\cite{gilardi2023chatgpt,tornberg2024chatgpt,ziems2024nlp,tan2024llm}.
When these labels serve as effect modifiers in heterogeneous treatment effect (HTE) analysis, the standard correction protocol\footnote{Throughout, ``debiasing'' refers to correction of annotation measurement-error bias, not algorithmic fairness debiasing.} is straightforward: validate a sample against expert annotations, estimate a confusion matrix, and apply its inverse uniformly to correct conditional average treatment effect (CATE) estimates.
Yet LLM annotations are not uniformly accurate: \citet{ashwin2025bias} document error rates varying by up to 5$\times$ across demographic subgroups; in our pilot, GPT-4.1 error rates range from 10.2\% to 20.0\% across text-feature subgroups ($\Delta\pi = 0.098$).

We show that when error rates vary across subgroups, this standard correction can make things worse.
In 14.9\% of our synthetic configurations (across a grid of $\Delta\pi$ and $\tau(0)$ values; $\tau(0) = 0.03$), global correction reverses the sign of estimated CATE---a protective treatment ($\tau = +0.03$) appears harmful ($\hat{\tau} = -0.059$)---rising to 31\% at $\tau(0) = 0.01$ (Figure~\ref{fig:sign_reversal}); all eight real LLMs tested amplify bias under global correction, while our per-subgroup correction (EC-HTE; Section~\ref{sec:method}) eliminates sign reversal in the primary $K{=}2$ setting.
Global correction amplifies subgroup-level bias by up to 2.86$\times$ ($K{=}2$) and 2.41$\times$ ($K{=}4$); the classical Frazis--Loewenstein attenuation correction~\cite{frazis2003estimating} \emph{increases} bias by up to 2.9$\times$.
The mechanism is over-correction: pooled correction calibrated for the average error rate introduces substantial bias in low-error subgroups where the naive estimator is nearly unbiased.

We derive an exact bias identity explaining when and why (Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity}), verified at $R^2 \geq 0.97$ on synthetic grids.
The per-subgroup correction prescribed by the identity reduces $|\text{bias}|$ by $7.4\times$ under extreme heterogeneity; the advantage is conditional on sufficient $\Delta\pi$ and expert budget.

Existing corrections---prediction-powered inference (PPI)~\cite{angelopoulos2023ppi}, design-based supervised learning (DSL)~\cite{egami2023dsl}, prediction-powered conditional inference (PPCI)~\cite{sui2026ppci}, arm-specific PPI~\cite{mozer2026ppi}, ForestIV~\cite{bu2023forestiv}---assume homogeneous prediction error; none addresses effect modifier misclassification (Section~\ref{sec:related}).

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig1_sign_reversal.pdf}
\caption{CATE sign reversal under global correction ($K{=}2$, $\tau(0) = 0.03$). (A) Naive vs.\ global-corrected CATE; red triangles mark sign reversals (7.5\% of subgroup estimates). (B) Extreme case: true $\tau = +0.03$, estimated $\hat{\tau} = -0.059$. (C) 14.9\% of configurations exhibit sign reversal; all 8 real LLMs amplify bias; EC-HTE: 0\% reversal.}
\label{fig:sign_reversal}
\end{figure*}

\begin{figure*}[!t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig_combined_overview.pdf}
\caption{Overview. (a)~LLM classifiers exhibit heterogeneous error rates across subgroups (up to $5\times$). (b)~Global correction over/under-corrects, introducing bias $\delta_s \cdot \Delta\tau / (1{-}2\bar{\pi})$ that can reverse CATE sign. (c)~\textit{Top (rose):} standard pipeline applies a pooled confusion matrix uniformly; \textit{bottom (teal):} EC-HTE estimates per-subgroup matrices via hierarchical Bayes, applies stratified inversion with a $\kappa$-guard, and propagates uncertainty into CausalForestDML. (d)~Key results across eight LLMs and four NLP tasks.}
\label{fig:overview}
\label{fig:method_overview}
\end{figure*}

Our contributions are:
\begin{itemize}
    \item An exact bias identity (Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity}): global correction introduces subgroup-level bias $\delta_s \cdot \Delta\tau / (1-2\bar{\pi})$, explaining when and why it reverses CATE sign; verified at $R^2 \geq 0.97$ ($R^2 = 0.975$ across 11 LLM--dataset combinations). The identity yields a $\Delta\tau$-free diagnostic (Proposition~\ref{prop:debiasing_hurts}) predicting when correction increases bias from error rates alone.
    \item Empirical operating guidelines: per-subgroup correction outperforms global correction when $\Delta\pi > 0.15$, $n_{\text{expert}} \geq 250$, and $K \leq 4$ subgroups; outside this envelope, the diagnostic guides practitioners to retain standard correction.
    \item One concrete instantiation: hierarchical Bayesian confusion matrix estimation (EC-HTE), verified for DIM, CausalForestDML, and R-learner, and composable with PPCI for joint covariate/outcome error.
\end{itemize}

\section{Correction Prescribed by the Identity}
\label{sec:method}

Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity} identifies per-subgroup misclassification deviation as the bias source; the natural correction is per-subgroup confusion matrix inversion.
This correction (EC-HTE) proceeds in three stages: estimate per-subgroup confusion matrices via hierarchical Bayes, invert them to correct observed outcome moments, and propagate correction uncertainty into downstream causal estimators.
The complete procedure is summarized in Algorithm~\ref{alg:echte} (Appendix~\ref{app:algorithm}) and illustrated in Figure~\ref{fig:method_overview}.

\subsection{Hierarchical Bayes Confusion Matrix Estimation}

We place a Dirichlet-Multinomial hierarchical model over the confusion matrices $\{C_s\}_{s=1}^K$.
The model estimates the full $J \times J$ confusion matrix per subgroup and does not require symmetric error rates~(A2); (A2) simplifies only the diagnostic (Proposition~\ref{prop:debiasing_hurts}).
For each subgroup $s$, row $j$ of $C_s$ has a Dirichlet prior centered at the global pooled confusion matrix $\hat{C}_{\text{global}}$: $\text{Dir}(\alpha_0 \cdot \hat{C}_{\text{global},j})$, where $\hat{C}_{\text{global},j}$ is the $j$-th row of the pooled CM and $\alpha_0 > 0$ is a shared concentration parameter estimated via empirical Bayes (marginal likelihood maximization).
We place a $\mathrm{Gamma}(3, 1)$ hyperprior on $\alpha_0$, robust to $2\times$ perturbation (Appendix~\ref{app:hyperprior}).
Large $\alpha_0$ pulls estimates toward $\hat{C}_{\text{global}}$; small $\alpha_0$ allows subgroup-specific rates to dominate---partial pooling borrows strength while preserving heterogeneity.

Posterior inference uses empirical Bayes optimization ($<$5ms; Markov chain Monte Carlo (MCMC) alternative in Appendix~\ref{app:mcmc}).
The key design choice is hierarchical partial pooling rather than independent per-subgroup estimation: when expert labels are scarce relative to the number of subgroups ($n_{\text{expert}} / K < 100$), per-subgroup MLE suffers high variance from small cell counts, while global pooling discards the heterogeneity that Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity} identifies as the bias source.
HB estimation outperforms stratified MLE in 87.5\% of configurations (Section~\ref{sec:ec_hte_eval}) by adaptively interpolating between these extremes; below $n_{\text{expert}} \times P(Z_{\text{minority}}) / K < 25$, stratified MLE is preferred.

\subsection{Subgroup-Stratified Correction}

Given posterior mean confusion matrices $\hat{C}_s$, we correct observed outcome moments per subgroup by matrix inversion:
\begin{equation}
\tilde{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_s = \hat{C}_s^{-1} \cdot \hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_s,
\end{equation}
where $\hat{\boldsymbol{\mu}}_s = [\hat{\tau}_{\text{obs}}(0, s),\, \hat{\tau}_{\text{obs}}(1, s)]^\top$ collects the observed conditional outcome moments in subgroup $s$.
Invertibility requires $\pi_s \neq 0.5$ (A4); Laplace smoothing and a $\kappa$-guard ($\kappa(\hat{C}_s) > 100 \to$ fallback to $\bar{C}^{-1}$; threshold ablation in Appendix~\ref{app:kappa_ablation}) ensure finite-sample stability.
All 13 tested LLM--dataset combinations fall in $\kappa \in (1.25, 3]$ with coverage $\geq$93\% (Appendix~\ref{app:kappa_analysis}).

Global correction applies $\bar{C}^{-1}$ uniformly, introducing the $\delta_s \cdot \Delta\tau$ bias of Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity}; subgroup-stratified correction eliminates this at the cost of higher variance (Appendix~\ref{sec:budget}).

\subsection{Downstream Integration}

Corrected covariates are fed into CausalForestDML~\cite{wager2018causal} with honest splitting; bias corrections propagate as weighted averages of per-subgroup corrections ($R^2 > 0.97$ across 520 configurations; Appendix~\ref{app:bootstrap_se}).
Standard errors are adjusted via $\text{se}_{\text{corrected},i} = \sqrt{\text{diag}(\hat{C}_{S_i}^{-1}\, \text{diag}(\text{se}_i^2)\, \hat{C}_{S_i}^{-\top})}$; below $n_{\text{expert}}/K < 50$, a bootstrap ($B{=}100$) maintains $\geq$95\% coverage (Appendix~\ref{app:bootstrap_se}).

\paragraph{Diagnostic criterion.}
Proposition~\ref{prop:debiasing_hurts} provides a $\Delta\tau$-free diagnostic: global correction harms subgroup $s$ iff $|\delta_s| / ((1-2\bar{\pi}) \cdot \pi_s) > 1$, computable from per-subgroup error rates alone (AUC~$= 0.86$; Section~\ref{sec:diagnostic}).

\paragraph{Composition with PPCI.}
EC-HTE corrects covariate misclassification; PPCI~\cite{sui2026ppci} corrects outcome-side error. Under both error types, composition reduces $|\text{bias}|$ from 0.179 to 0.007, valid when error sources are independent (Appendix~\ref{sec:ppci}).

\section{Experiments}

\begin{figure}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\columnwidth]{figures/fig4_budget_compact.pdf}
\caption{RMSE vs.\ expert budget under extreme heterogeneity. Crossover at $n{=}250$ ($K{=}2$) and $n{=}1000$ ($K{=}4$, $\Delta\tau{=}0.3$); under the Table~\ref{tab:main_results} DGP, $K{=}4$ crossover is lower ($n{\approx}500$). Shaded bands: $\pm 1$ SD. Full grid in Appendix~\ref{app:budget}.}
\label{fig:budget_compact}
\end{figure}

\subsection{Setup}

We test on synthetic ($N{=}5{,}000$, $K \in \{2, 4\}$, controlled $\Delta\tau$ and $\Delta\pi$, 100 Monte Carlo (MC) runs) and semi-synthetic data-generating processes (DGPs) spanning four NLP tasks---sentiment analysis (TweetEval~\cite{barbieri2020tweeteval}, $K{=}4$), hate speech detection (CivilComments, $K{=}4$), stance detection ($K{=}5$; Appendix~\ref{app:stance}), and topic classification (AG News, $K{=}4$; Appendix~\ref{app:agnews})---with full specifications in Appendix~\ref{app:dgp}.

We compare: (1) \textit{Naive} (no correction), (2) \textit{Global} (pooled confusion matrix, equivalent to PPI/DSL~\cite{angelopoulos2023ppi,egami2023dsl} with homogeneous error), (3) \textit{Stratified MLE} (per-subgroup DSL without shrinkage), (4) \textit{HB EC-HTE} (per-subgroup correction), (5) \textit{PPCI}~\cite{sui2026ppci}, and (6) \textit{EC-HTE+PPCI}.
For CausalForest~\cite{wager2018causal}, we compare naive, global, and EC-HTE variants.
Primary metrics are $|\text{bias}|$, root mean squared error (RMSE), and 95\% confidence interval (CI) coverage, averaged across subgroups and 100 MC runs.
Robustness validations cover eight conditions including asymmetric errors, subgroup misspecification, and treatment-dependent misclassification (all $\geq$89.5\% coverage; Appendices~\ref{app:hatespeech}--\ref{app:treatment_dep}).
Semi-synthetic evaluation uses empirical confusion matrices from 11 LLM--dataset combinations (eight LLMs across two datasets; Appendix~\ref{app:realcm_bias}; semi-real CM validation in Appendix~\ref{app:semireal}; open-source LLM validation in Appendix~\ref{app:multilm_opensource}).

\subsection{Empirical Validation of the Bias Identity}
\label{sec:bias_mechanism}

\begin{figure*}[t]
\centering
\includegraphics[width=\textwidth]{figures/fig2_heatmap.pdf}
\caption{Bias amplification under global correction ($K{=}2$). (a)~High-error subgroup ($\pi_s > \bar{\pi}$): correction always reduces bias. (b)~Low-error subgroup ($\pi_s < \bar{\pi}$): correction \emph{increases} bias when $\Delta\pi > 0.12$ (hatched), reaching $2.86\times$.}
\label{fig:heatmap}
\end{figure*}

We verify Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity} on a $K{=}2$ grid (520 configurations, $\Delta\pi \in [0, 0.25]$, $\Delta\tau \in (0, 0.50]$) and a $K{=}4$ grid (Appendix~\ref{app:dgp}).
Regressing observed subgroup bias on $\delta_s \cdot \Delta\tau / (1{-}2\bar{\pi})$ yields $R^2 = 0.994$ ($K{=}2$) and $0.97$ ($K{=}4$); residual variance reflects MC noise ($R^2 \geq 0.976$ even at $P(Z{=}1) = 0.1$; Appendix~\ref{app:classimbalance}).
Global correction worsens bias in 50\% of $K{=}2$ configurations (260/520, excluding $\Delta\pi{=}0$), with amplification reaching 2.86$\times$ ($K{=}2$) and 2.41$\times$ ($K{=}4$); Figure~\ref{fig:heatmap} visualizes the amplification surface.
Global correction also reverses the sign of estimated CATE in 14.9\% of configurations at $\tau(0) = 0.03$, with all eight real LLMs amplifying bias; EC-HTE eliminates sign reversal (Figure~\ref{fig:sign_reversal}; Appendix~\ref{app:policy_reversal}).
Cross-task validation on stance detection ($K{=}5$, Appendix~\ref{app:stance}) confirms sign reversal (13.3\% $\to$ 3.3\%) and reveals limitations when FNR${}>0.50$.
On CivilComments hate speech detection (Appendix~\ref{app:civilcomments}), the bias identity changes the substantive NLP conclusion: global correction suggests demographic-group moderation effects are uniformly small, while EC-HTE reveals a 3.2$\times$ effect-size gap between identity subgroups---consistent with known annotation disparities~\cite{borkan2019nuanced} (Appendix~\ref{app:hatespeech}).
Researchers using global correction would miss this differential impact entirely.

\subsection{Correction Evaluation}
\label{sec:ec_hte_eval}

Table~\ref{tab:main_results} presents the main results.
In the $K{=}2$ extreme row, global correction \emph{increases} $|\text{bias}|$ relative to naive (0.059 vs.\ 0.058), confirming Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity}; F-L corrections fare worse (0.122--0.168).
HB EC-HTE reduces $|\text{bias}|$ from 0.059 to 0.008 (7.4$\times$); relative to stratified MLE (0.012), the advantage is 1.5$\times$ with lower RMSE from partial pooling. At $K{=}4$, bias reduces from 0.056 to 0.012 (4.7$\times$), maintaining near-nominal coverage (0.94--0.96).
Under moderate heterogeneity ($\Delta\pi \leq 0.10$), methods converge ($p > 0.05$; Table~\ref{tab:main_supp}).
EC-HTE requires $n_{\text{expert}} \geq 250$ ($K{=}2$) or $\geq 500$ ($K{=}4$); below this, RMSE exceeds global (Figure~\ref{fig:budget_compact}; HB vs.\ stratified MLE budget sensitivity in Appendix~\ref{app:budget_sensitivity}; $K{=}8$ analysis in Appendix~\ref{app:k8_subgroups}).

\paragraph{CausalForest.}
CausalForest~\cite{wager2018causal} with a noisy $\hat{Z}$ inherits the same $M_{\text{global}}^{-1} M_s$ structure.
EC-HTE reduces $|\text{bias}|$ from 0.191 to 0.039 (4.9$\times$) with coverage 0.90 ($K{=}4$, $n{=}500$; oracle coverage decomposition in Appendix~\ref{app:cf_oracle}); R-learner yields comparable reduction (89\%, Appendix~\ref{app:rlearner}), confirming the bias arises from the confusion matrix structure, not the downstream estimator.

\paragraph{PPCI composition.}
When both the effect modifier and the outcome are LLM-annotated, EC-HTE (covariate correction) composes with PPCI~\cite{sui2026ppci} (outcome correction).
Under joint misclassification, EC-HTE+PPCI reduces $|\text{bias}|$ from 0.179 (naive) to 0.007, compared to 0.092 for PPCI alone and 0.064 for EC-HTE alone, with coverage $\geq$94\% (Appendix~\ref{sec:ppci}).
The composition is valid when covariate and outcome errors are independent; practitioners should verify this when both variables are LLM-annotated.

\begin{table}[t]
\centering
\caption{Main results (extreme heterogeneity). Metrics averaged over subgroups, $z$-levels, 100 MC runs. Bold: lowest $|\text{bias}|$. $^\dagger$RMSE exceeds Global. F-L Scalar/Per-sub.: \citet{frazis2003estimating}. Moderate/TweetEval in Table~\ref{tab:main_supp}; MC SEs $\leq 0.005$ (Appendix~\ref{app:mcse}).\protect\footnotemark}
\label{tab:main_results}
