\subsection{Diagnostic and Operating Conditions}
\label{sec:diagnostic}

Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity} operates on the full matrix residual $B_s = M_{\text{global}}^{-1} M_s - I$; the scalar diagnostic below is its (A2)-simplified form.
Proposition~\ref{prop:debiasing_hurts} provides a $\Delta\tau$-free diagnostic: global correction increases bias in subgroup $s$ iff $|\delta_s| / ((1-2\bar{\pi}) \cdot \pi_s) > 1$, computable from a pilot sample without estimating treatment effects (AUC $= 0.86$ symmetric, $0.96$ asymmetric; ROC curves in Appendix~\ref{app:roc}; screening details in Appendix~\ref{app:screening}).
Under relaxed screening, 79\% of real LLM--dataset pairs show RMSE improvement with coverage $\geq$93\% (Appendix~\ref{app:screening}).

\paragraph{Operating conditions.}
EC-HTE requires: (i)~$\Delta\pi > 0.15$, (ii)~$n_{\text{expert}} \geq 250$ for $K{=}2$, (iii)~FNR${}<0.50$, (iv)~$K \leq 4$, and (v)~$n_{\text{expert}} \geq 500$ under asymmetric errors (FPR$\neq$FNR); when unmet, global correction is preferable (Appendix~\ref{app:k8_subgroups}).
Robustness checks (noisy labels, confounded treatment assignment (Appendix~\ref{app:confounded_dgp}), joint assumption violations) appear in Appendices~\ref{app:robustness}--\ref{app:multilm}.
Across 11 real LLM--dataset combinations (eight LLMs including GPT-4.1, Claude 3.5, Gemini 2.5 Flash, and five open-source models; Appendix~\ref{app:multilm_opensource}), the bias identity achieves aggregate $R^2 = 0.975$ (per-model range 0.76--0.99), and EC-HTE improves RMSE over global correction in 79\% of screening configurations with coverage $\geq$93\%.

\section{Related Work}
\label{sec:related}

Classical measurement error correction can amplify MSE under misspecification~\cite{fuller1987measurement,carroll2006measurement}; \citet{frazis2003estimating} derive scalar attenuation for homogeneous errors, which Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity} generalizes to heterogeneous settings.
HTE estimators~\cite{athey2016recursive,wager2018causal,chernozhukov2018dml} assume error-free covariates; LLM error rates vary by up to $5\times$ across subgroups~\cite{ashwin2025bias}.
PPI~\cite{angelopoulos2023ppi}, DSL~\cite{egami2023dsl}, PPCI~\cite{sui2026ppci}, and ForestIV~\cite{bu2023forestiv} correct population-level or outcome-side error assuming homogeneous prediction quality; none addresses effect modifier misclassification.
Extended discussion appears in Appendix~\ref{app:related}.

