Empirical verification yields $R^2 = 0.994$ ($K{=}2$) and $0.97$ ($K{=}4$; Section~\ref{sec:bias_mechanism}).

\paragraph{Sensitivity to (A1).}
Assumption~(A1) is not empirically testable; if misclassification correlates with potential outcomes, Theorem~\ref{thm:bias_identity} gains an $O(\gamma)$ perturbation.
At $\gamma_T = 0.10$, coverage degrades to 75\% (Appendix~\ref{app:treatment_dep}); sensitivity analysis for outcome-dependent misclassification appears in Appendix~\ref{app:a1_sensitivity}. Sensitivity frameworks~\cite{lash2009applying,dorn2022sharp,cinelli2020making} can bound this.

\paragraph{Finite-sample remainder.}
With estimated $\hat{C}_s = C_s + E_s$, $\|E_s\| = O_p(n_{\text{expert}}^{-1/2})$, the remainder is $R_n = O_p(n_{\text{expert}}^{-1/2} \cdot \kappa(C_s)^2)$, where the squared condition number arises from the Neumann expansion $\hat{C}_s^{-1} = C_s^{-1} - C_s^{-1} E_s C_s^{-1} + O(\|E_s\|^2)$, motivating the $\kappa$-guard in Section~\ref{sec:method}.

\begin{proposition}[Debiasing Can Increase Bias]
\label{prop:debiasing_hurts}
Under \textnormal{(A1)--(A5)}, global correction produces larger absolute bias than the naive estimator ($|\mathrm{bias}_{\mathrm{global}}| > |\mathrm{bias}_{\mathrm{naive}}|$) in subgroup $s$ if and only if
\begin{equation}
\frac{|\delta_s|}{(1 - 2\bar{\pi}) \cdot \pi_s} > 1.
\label{eq:hurts_condition}
\end{equation}
\end{proposition}

\medskip
Equation~\eqref{eq:hurts_condition} partitions subgroups into two regimes:
\begin{itemize}
    \item \textit{High-error} ($\pi_s > \bar{\pi}$): the condition reduces to $\pi_s > 0.5$, excluded by~(A4); global correction always reduces bias.
    \item \textit{Low-error} ($\pi_s < \bar{\pi}$): the condition holds whenever $\pi_s < \bar{\pi} / (2(1 - \bar{\pi}))$---easily satisfied: when $\bar{\pi} = 0.15$, any subgroup with $\pi_s < 0.088$ is harmed by global correction.
\end{itemize}
The mechanism is over-correction: for subgroups with near-zero misclassification ($\pi_s \approx 0$), the naive estimator is nearly unbiased, yet global inversion calibrated for $\bar{\pi}$ creates substantial bias---debiasing is worse than doing nothing, and can reverse bias sign across subgroups (proof in Appendix~\ref{app:debiasing_hurts}).

In our experiments (Section~\ref{sec:bias_mechanism}), global correction increases bias in 50\% of $K{=}2$ configurations (260/520), with amplification reaching $2.86\times$ ($K{=}2$) and $2.41\times$ ($K{=}4$); Figure~\ref{fig:heatmap} visualizes the full parameter grid.

